          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Sand primarily signifies an innumerable multitude and, in the building parable, an unstable or inadequate foundation. Other context-dependent senses are also recognized.\",\"book_summary\":\"Sand signifies a countless multitude; as a foundation, it represents the instability of relying on a crowd of people not joined by covenant rather than on the covenant Rock.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Innumerable multitude; secondarily, an unstable foundation.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Countless multitude; when foundational, an unstable crowd outside covenant in contrast with Rock.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The broad identifications match, but the book specifically reclassifies the foundation image as reliance on an uncovenanted crowd. The consensus instead associates Matthew 7 more generally with hearing without obedience or any inadequate foundation and does not impose the crowd/covenant boundary.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no contradiction at the minimum core: both identify multitude and foundational instability. The book's covenant-crowd explanation is a materially narrower interpretive specification rather than a replacement of those core meanings.\",\"rationale\":\"The multitude sense directly matches the consensus, and the unstable-foundation sense is also recognized by all responses. The entry is REFINED because it connects these senses and gives the sand-foundation a specific human and covenantal referent not contained in the consensus formulation.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Genesis 22:17, Hosea 1:10, and Revelation 20:8 strongly support innumerability, while Matthew 7:26 supports an unsuccessful sand foundation. However, Matthew identifies the foolish builder as one who hears Jesus' sayings and does not do them; it does not itself identify the sand as a crowd, uncovenanted people, or the opposite of a covenant Rock.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
